Digital Sovereignty on a European Scale in times of increased global competition

The issue of ‘Digital Sovereignty’ has been referred to by different EU actors and member states’ governments for several years and is only gaining more high-profile traction amongst European lawmakers (Roberts et al., 2021, p. 4). The European Commission under Ursula von der Leyen, the European Council under Charles Michel, the German Presidency of the Council of the European Union and lastly the European Parliament have all adopted the term and have used it in their official communication and policy proposals (German Presidency of the Council of the European Union, 2020; Madiega, 2020; Michel, 2021; von der Leyen, 2020) – without adopting a common definition and thus leading to varying interpretations and implementations of EU efforts to achieve Digital Sovereignty (Roberts et al., 2021, p. 18). If the EU is to be successful in its drive towards achieving Digital Sovereignty, it not only needs to adopt policy frameworks that enhance this endeavour but also needs to adopt a common definition for what it means by ‘Digital Sovereignty’.

‘Digital Sovereignty’ should be understood as a form of legitimate, controlling authority which is applied outside of the traditional conceptualisation of sovereignty with the nation-state (Pohle & Thiel, 2020, pp. 3, 11). It should also be extended to the process of governance – by supranational institutions, nation-states and transnational companies alike – to encompass the efforts undertaken to achieve ‘Digital Sovereignty’ because the digital “governance process involves the exercise of the capacities afforded, a priori, by sovereignty” (Roberts et al., 2021, p. 7).

The danger posed by the EU’s main competitors in the economic field, the US and China, has only increased since the current Trump administration indicated their position on European Digital Sovereignty by sanctioning one of the key figures behind the EU’s ‘Digital Services Act’ (DSA) and open support for far-right actors in the EU (Politico Europe, 2025; Satariano et al., 2025). 

Both the US and China have also established themselves as key players in the AI market, with European actors so far not being successful in establishing themselves as significant actors. A last point of context relates to the alliance formed between major US-based technology firms (GAFAM) and the current US administration which serves to decrease the threat posed to these companies’ interests by EU regulation like the DSA and the ‘Digital Markets Act’ (DMA) (Echikson, 2025, p. 67; European Commission, 2025a; Regulation 2022/2065 (Digital Services Act), 2022).

The DSA and DMA are essential parts of the puzzle that is achieving EU Digital Sovereignty. The US’ push-back against this EU legislature shows not only that the EU finds itself on a collision-course with US interests but also that the EU’s ability to govern the global digital market – as was the case with the GDPR – remains effective. Next to breaking the dominant role of digital intermediaries and platforms, ensuring the correct payment of profits and the upholding of open internet protocols that digital communications rely upon, the DSA and DMA increase the EU’s ability to exercise control over public deliberation and ensuring democratic sovereignty against the current US push towards far-right ideologies in the EU fostered by US-based tech firms such as Meta (Echikson, 2025, p. 68).

The adoption of the DSA and progress on the DMA have already increased the EU’s ability to achieve Digital Sovereignty. However, more is needed to systematically achieve Digital Sovereignty in the EU. The pooling of European resources should be considered as the EU’s greatest strength and some earlier efforts to support the pooling of digital resources have been conceptualised, most notably Gaia-X which did not fulfil the expectations towards it. Gaia-X’s implementation as a non-profit which organised private and public cloud-providers turned out to be too inefficient and Amazon as well as Google were directly involved in its establishment, effectively diluting the impact that Gaia-X could have had on transforming the European cloud-computing sector.

What is needed is the establishment of other pooling programmes provided with sufficient funds to shift European digital infrastructure towards an open-source model while also creating a financial environment in which code-contributors can be effectively reimbursed for their contribution. It is important that the EU is directly involved in such a programme, as the current geopolitical situation calls for a swift transition towards strategic autonomy. We find ourselves at a critical juncture, and given that the European trajectory towards autonomy, reached by embracing open-source technologies, is already taking shape, securing mid- and long-term financial and administrative security for open-source programmes and stacks is of utmost importance. One such example is the EU’s SIMPL programme. The programme embraces a community-driven approach to European data-sharing based on open-source programming. In essence, SIMPL shares some of Gaia-X’s visions relating to the pooling of existing European data resources whilst improving on Gaia-X’s original issues – primarily a lack of community involvement paired with the influence of Amazon and Google (Roberts et al., 2021, p. 15). 

 
Further, SIMPL – as a singular project – needs to be contextualised together with the European Commission’s DG CONNECT and their ‘Digital Europe’ programme as well as ‘Eurocloud’ which are all state-driven and funded but have the potential to include European community members in their processes. Whilst they have similar issues as the German-started ‘GovStack’ – namely its state-centric funding model – EU-level programmes provide the institutions with agenda-setting power and help implement a single definition of digital sovereignty needed on a supra-national and national level. SIMPL’s model of providing funds to contributors can also be emulated by other initiatives run or funded by the European Commission to ensure reliability and incentives for open-source contributors whilst retaining some elements of state-control vis-à-vis cybersecurity threats.

Lastly, the EU needs to combine efforts to legislate the existing digital markets, as done with the DSA and the upcoming DMA, follow down a path of common understanding of how Digital Sovereignty on a European level is to be achieved and implement programmes following SIMPL’s formula. Another question remains relating to how SIMPL will be accepted by the open-source community. The programme’s efforts to attract interest of community members by hosting an annual community event in Brussels based on exchange of information lets one hope that the mistakes of Gaia-X have been understood by the Commission and that SIMPL serves as a model to emulate by other European open-source initiatives (European Commission, 2025b).
 
